# Part 3: Machine Learning Algorithms & How Models Learn

## Introduction

The third video examines several machine learning models and algorithms and explains how models learn patterns from data.

The video demonstrates that there is no single machine learning model that is appropriate for every problem.

The appropriate model depends on factors such as:

- The problem being solved
- The available data
- The type of information being processed
- The requirements of the application

## Models and Algorithms Covered

- GPT
- BERT
- Recurrent Neural Networks
- ResNet
- Support Vector Machines
- WaveNet
- Generative Adversarial Networks
- XGBoost
- Neural Networks

## GPT

GPT stands for Generative Pre-trained Transformer.

GPT models are associated with generative AI and transformer-based language models.

They can generate text and computer code based on input prompts.

## BERT

BERT is a transformer-based language model designed for understanding language.

BERT processes information bidirectionally, allowing it to consider context from both directions when processing text.

## Recurrent Neural Networks

Recurrent Neural Networks, or RNNs, are designed for sequential data where the order of information is important.

Examples include:

- Time-series data
- Text
- Speech

## ResNet

ResNet is a deep neural network associated with image recognition.

Applications can include:

- Image recognition
- Object detection
- Facial recognition

## Support Vector Machines

Support Vector Machines, or SVMs, are machine learning algorithms that can be used for classification and regression.

SVMs provide an approach to machine learning that does not depend on the same neural-network architectures used by deep learning models.

## WaveNet

WaveNet is a model designed to generate raw audio waveforms.

It demonstrates how machine learning models can be applied to audio generation and speech synthesis.

## Generative Adversarial Networks

Generative Adversarial Networks, or GANs, can generate synthetic data that resembles the training data.

Potential applications include generating:

- Images
- Videos
- Sounds

GANs can also be used for data augmentation.

## XGBoost

XGBoost is an implementation of gradient boosting.

Gradient boosting combines models to improve predictive performance.

XGBoost demonstrates that machine learning includes approaches beyond deep neural networks.

